# Source for q/spi-transducers.js: Transducers and Resolution.
T="Transducers"; R="Resolution"
Q=[
# --- Piezoelectric effect and construction ---
(T,"The piezoelectric effect is the conversion of:","Pressure (mechanical) energy into electrical energy, and the reverse",["Heat into sound","Light into electricity","Magnetic energy into sound"],"A piezoelectric crystal produces a voltage when squeezed by a returning echo and vibrates when a voltage is applied, so one crystal both sends and receives.","F"),
(T,"The most common piezoelectric material in medical transducers is:","Lead zirconate titanate (PZT)",["Quartz","Tungsten","Epoxy resin"],"PZT is a manufactured ceramic. Quartz and tourmaline are natural piezoelectric materials but are not used in modern imaging transducers.","F"),
(T,"Heating a PZT crystal above its Curie temperature will:","Depolarize it so it loses its piezoelectric properties",["Raise its resonant frequency","Make it more sensitive","Increase its bandwidth"],"Above the Curie point (about 300 °C or more for PZT) the crystal's dipoles lose alignment. This is why transducers must never be heat-sterilized.","F"),
(T,"Polarization of a PZT crystal during manufacture is done by:","Heating it in a strong electric field and then cooling it",["Grinding it to quarter-wavelength thickness","Coating it with gel","Freezing it"],"The strong field aligns the dipoles while the material is hot; cooling locks them in place, making it piezoelectric.","C"),
(T,"For a pulsed-wave transducer, the resonant (main) frequency is determined by:","Crystal thickness and the speed of sound in the crystal",["The voltage applied by the machine","The PRF","The matching layer only"],"Frequency = speed of sound in PZT ÷ (2 × thickness). Thinner crystals ring at higher frequencies.","F"),
(T,"Which crystal produces the highest-frequency pulsed transducer?","The thinnest crystal",["The thickest crystal","The widest crystal","The narrowest crystal"],"The crystal thickness equals half the wavelength in the crystal, so a thinner crystal gives a shorter wavelength and higher frequency.","F"),
(T,"The frequency of a continuous-wave transducer equals:","The frequency of the electrical signal driving it",["The crystal's natural resonant frequency only","Half the PRF","The frequency set by the backing material"],"In CW the crystal is driven continuously, so it vibrates at the frequency of the electrical drive voltage.","C"),
(T,"Speed of sound in PZT is about 4.6 mm/µs. What crystal thickness gives a 4.6 MHz transducer?","0.5 mm",["1 mm","0.25 mm","2 mm"],"f = c ÷ (2 × thickness). Thickness = 4.6 ÷ (2 × 4.6) = 0.5 mm.","C"),
(T,"The backing (damping) material is bonded to the back of the crystal to:","Shorten the pulse by reducing ringing",["Increase the crystal's sensitivity","Match impedance with the skin","Protect the patient from electric shock"],"Damping stops the crystal ringing quickly, so the pulse has fewer cycles, giving a shorter pulse and better axial resolution.","F"),
(T,"Adding more damping to an imaging transducer will:","Shorten the pulse, widen the bandwidth and lower sensitivity",["Lengthen the pulse and narrow the bandwidth","Increase sensitivity and Q factor","Raise the main frequency"],"Damping trades sensitivity for shorter pulses. Short pulses contain a wide range of frequencies, so bandwidth increases and Q falls.","F"),
(T,"The backing material is commonly made of:","Epoxy resin loaded with tungsten",["Pure PZT","Gel","Air"],"Tungsten-loaded epoxy has a high impedance and absorbs the crystal's backward vibration.","F"),
(T,"Imaging transducers are described as low-Q because they have:","A wide bandwidth relative to their main frequency",["A narrow bandwidth","No damping","A very long pulse"],"Q factor = main frequency ÷ bandwidth. Damped imaging transducers have wide bandwidth and low Q (about 1 to 3).","F"),
(T,"Q factor is calculated as:","Main frequency ÷ bandwidth",["Bandwidth ÷ main frequency","Main frequency × bandwidth","PRF ÷ bandwidth"],"A 6 MHz transducer with a bandwidth of 3 MHz has a Q of 2.","F"),
(T,"A transducer has a main frequency of 5 MHz and a bandwidth of 2.5 MHz. What is its Q factor?","2",["0.5","12.5","7.5"],"Q = 5 ÷ 2.5 = 2.","C"),
(T,"Continuous-wave Doppler transducers are typically:","Undamped, with a narrow bandwidth and high Q",["Heavily damped, with a wide bandwidth","Single-crystal pulsed transducers","Low sensitivity"],"CW does not need short pulses, so the crystals are left undamped for maximum sensitivity and a narrow frequency range.","C"),
(T,"The matching layer sits:","Between the crystal and the skin",["Behind the crystal","Inside the backing material","Between the transducer and the cable"],"The matching layer reduces the large impedance difference between the crystal and skin, so more sound passes in and out.","F"),
(T,"The impedance of the matching layer should be:","Between the impedance of PZT and that of skin",["Higher than PZT","Lower than skin","Equal to air"],"An intermediate impedance steps sound across the boundary; PZT is about 20 times more impedant than skin.","F"),
(T,"Coupling gel is needed because:","Air between the transducer and skin would reflect almost all the sound",["Gel raises the transducer frequency","Gel focuses the beam","Gel cools the crystal"],"Air has a tiny impedance compared with tissue, so an air gap causes near-total reflection. Gel removes it.","F"),
(T,"The protective case of a transducer includes an electrical shield and an acoustic insulator. The acoustic insulator:","Stops vibrations in the case from inducing signals in the crystal",["Matches impedance with skin","Sets the resonant frequency","Focuses the beam"],"A barrier of cork or rubber isolates the crystal from vibration of the housing.","C"),
(T,"A broadband transducer is useful because:","It can operate at several frequencies and supports harmonic imaging",["It has a very high Q","It only works in CW mode","It needs no matching layer"],"Wide-bandwidth transducers can switch between frequencies and receive harmonic frequencies, such as twice the transmitted frequency.","F"),
# --- Beam anatomy ---
(T,"The region of the beam from the transducer face to the focus is called the:","Near zone (Fresnel zone)",["Far zone (Fraunhofer zone)","Focal zone","Side lobe"],"The beam converges in the near zone, narrowing to its focus, then diverges in the far zone.","F"),
(T,"In an unfocused single-element transducer, beam width at the focus is about:","Half the transducer diameter",["Equal to the transducer diameter","Twice the transducer diameter","One quarter of the transducer diameter"],"At the end of the near zone the beam is half as wide as the crystal. At two near-zone lengths it is back to the crystal's width.","F"),
(T,"At a depth of two near-zone lengths, an unfocused beam is about:","As wide as the transducer diameter",["Half the transducer diameter","Twice the transducer diameter","Zero width"],"The beam widens again in the far zone and reaches the original diameter at twice the near-zone length.","F"),
(T,"Which change increases the near-zone (focal) length of an unfocused transducer?","A larger diameter or a higher frequency",["A smaller diameter","A lower frequency","More damping"],"Near-zone length = D² ÷ 4λ. Larger diameter and shorter wavelength (higher frequency) both push the focus deeper.","F"),
(T,"Beam divergence in the far zone is reduced by:","A larger diameter and a higher frequency",["A smaller diameter and a lower frequency","Increasing the PRF","Using less damping"],"The same factors that lengthen the near zone also make the far field spread less.","F"),
(T,"A 6 mm diameter unfocused transducer has a beam width at its focus of about:","3 mm",["6 mm","12 mm","1.5 mm"],"At the focus the beam is half the crystal diameter: 6 ÷ 2 = 3 mm.","F"),
(T,"Fixed (conventional) focusing with a lens or curved crystal will:","Narrow the beam in the focal zone and move the focus shallower",["Move the focus deeper","Widen the beam everywhere","Increase the near-zone length"],"Focusing makes the near zone shorter and the beam narrower around the focus, improving lateral resolution there.","F"),
(T,"Side lobes are created by:","Single-element transducers, from vibration of the crystal edges",["Array transducers only","High PRF","The backing material"],"Side lobes come from single crystals. Grating lobes are the equivalent extra beams from array transducers.","C"),
(T,"Grating lobes are associated with:","Array transducers, because of the regular spacing of elements",["Single-element transducers","Continuous-wave transducers only","Annular arrays only"],"Grating lobes are reduced by subdicing elements and by apodization.","F"),
(T,"Apodization reduces grating lobes by:","Driving the outer elements with lower voltages than the inner ones",["Cutting elements into smaller pieces","Adding more backing material","Increasing the PRF"],"Tapering the excitation across the aperture lowers the energy in off-axis lobes.","C"),
(T,"Subdicing reduces grating lobes by:","Dividing each element into smaller pieces",["Lowering voltage to outer elements","Adding a second matching layer","Using a lower frequency"],"Smaller sub-elements change the spacing that causes grating lobes.","C"),
# --- Arrays ---
(T,"A linear sequential array produces an image that is:","Rectangular",["Sector (pie) shaped","Circular","Trapezoidal only"],"Groups of elements fire in sequence along the face, each sending a straight scan line, forming a rectangle.","F"),
(T,"A curvilinear (convex) array produces an image that is:","A blunted sector, flat at the top",["Rectangular","A pointed sector","Circular"],"The elements are arranged on a curved face, so scan lines fan out from a wide footprint.","F"),
(T,"Adult echocardiography usually uses a phased array because it:","Has a small footprint that fits between the ribs and steers the beam electronically",["Has the largest footprint","Produces a rectangular image","Has no electronic focusing"],"A phased array forms a sector image from a small face, ideal for imaging the heart through narrow rib spaces.","F"),
(T,"A phased array steers the beam by:","Firing the elements with small, progressive time delays",["Moving the crystal with a motor","Using a curved lens","Changing the crystal thickness"],"A sloped pattern of delays tilts the wavefront to steer. A curved pattern of delays focuses it.","F"),
(T,"In a phased array, a curved pattern of firing delays (outer elements first) produces:","Electronic focusing",["Steering only","Grating lobes","A rectangular image"],"Firing outer elements slightly before inner ones makes the wavefronts converge at a chosen depth.","F"),
(T,"If one element fails in a linear sequential array, the image shows:","A vertical line of dropout from top to bottom",["Poor steering across the whole image","No change at all","A horizontal band"],"Each element contributes to particular scan lines, so a dead element causes a vertical dropout.","F"),
(T,"If one element fails in a phased array, the result is usually:","Poor steering and focusing across the image",["A single vertical line of dropout","Complete loss of the image","A brighter image"],"All elements work together to form every scan line, so one failure degrades the whole beam rather than one line.","C"),
(T,"An annular array differs from a phased array because it:","Has ring-shaped elements, focuses electronically in all planes and is steered mechanically",["Steers electronically","Produces a rectangular image","Has a single element"],"Concentric rings allow focusing in both lateral and elevational planes, but the beam must be moved mechanically.","C"),
(T,"A vector array combines a linear array with:","Phased steering to create a trapezoidal image",["A mechanical motor","An annular ring","A continuous-wave crystal"],"Steering the end lines outward lets a linear array produce a flat-topped, wider trapezoidal image.","C"),
(T,"Dynamic receive focusing means:","The receive focus changes continuously as echoes return from deeper depths",["The transmit focus is fixed","The beam is steered mechanically","The PRF changes with depth"],"Receive delays are adjusted moment by moment so echoes from every depth are in focus.","C"),
(T,"Using multiple transmit focal zones will:","Improve lateral resolution over a longer depth but lower the frame rate",["Raise the frame rate","Worsen lateral resolution","Have no effect on frame rate"],"Each focal zone needs its own pulse per scan line, so more zones means longer frame times.","F"),
(T,"A 1.5D or 2D (matrix) array improves:","Elevational resolution (slice thickness)",["Axial resolution only","Temporal resolution","Doppler sensitivity only"],"Extra rows of elements allow focusing in the elevational plane, thinning the slice. Matrix arrays also allow real-time 3D.","F"),
(T,"Real-time 3D echocardiography is made possible by:","Matrix (2D) arrays with thousands of elements",["Single-element transducers","Linear sequential arrays","Continuous-wave probes"],"A matrix array steers the beam in both lateral and elevational directions to acquire a volume.","F"),
(T,"A transesophageal (TEE) probe gives better images of posterior structures because:","It sits close to the heart behind the left atrium, so a higher frequency can be used with little attenuation",["It uses a lower frequency","It images through the ribs","It uses continuous-wave only"],"Being close to the heart avoids the chest wall and lung, so 5 to 7 MHz can be used with good penetration.","F"),
(T,"The typical frequency range for an adult transthoracic echo transducer is:","About 2 to 4 MHz",["About 10 to 15 MHz","About 0.5 MHz","About 20 to 50 MHz"],"Low frequencies are needed to penetrate 15 to 24 cm. Pediatric probes use higher frequencies.","F"),
(T,"Harmonic imaging receives echoes at:","Twice the transmitted (fundamental) frequency",["Half the transmitted frequency","The transmitted frequency only","The PRF"],"Tissue distorts the sound wave as it travels, creating harmonics. Receiving the second harmonic improves resolution and reduces artifacts.","F"),
(T,"Tissue harmonic imaging reduces artifacts because harmonics:","Are generated mostly in the strong central beam, not in weak side lobes or the near field",["Are louder than the fundamental","Travel faster","Have no attenuation"],"Harmonic energy grows with beam intensity, so weak side lobes and reverberations near the skin create few harmonics.","C"),
(T,"Pulse inversion harmonic imaging works by:","Sending two pulses of opposite phase; the fundamentals cancel and harmonics remain",["Doubling the PRF","Using two transducers","Turning off the receiver"],"Adding the echoes of a normal and an inverted pulse cancels the linear signal and keeps the nonlinear harmonic signal.","C"),
(T,"Transducers should be cleaned and disinfected:","With methods approved by the manufacturer, never by heat sterilization",["By autoclaving","By boiling","By soaking the connector in water"],"Heat can exceed the Curie point and destroy the crystal. Follow the maker's approved disinfectants.","F"),
(T,"Mechanical transducers produce sector images by:","Moving a single crystal or group of crystals with a motor",["Firing elements with time delays","Using a matrix array","Using a fixed lens only"],"Steering is mechanical, and focusing is fixed. A damaged crystal affects the entire image.","F"),
# --- Resolution: axial ---
(R,"Axial resolution is the ability to separate two structures that lie:","One in front of the other, along the beam",["Side by side, perpendicular to the beam","Above and below the image plane","At the same depth"],"Axial means along the axis of the beam. It is also called longitudinal, range, radial or depth resolution (LARRD).","F"),
(R,"Axial resolution equals:","Half the spatial pulse length",["The beam width","Twice the wavelength","The slice thickness"],"Two reflectors are resolved if they are more than half a pulse length apart, so their echoes don't overlap.","F"),
(R,"Spatial pulse length equals:","Number of cycles in the pulse × wavelength",["PRF × wavelength","Frequency × beam width","Period × PRF"],"SPL is the physical length of one pulse in tissue.","F"),
(R,"In soft tissue, the wavelength of a 5 MHz pulse is about:","0.31 mm",["3.1 mm","1.54 mm","0.031 mm"],"λ (mm) = 1.54 ÷ f (MHz) = 1.54 ÷ 5 ≈ 0.31 mm.","F"),
(R,"A 5 MHz pulse has 2 cycles. What is its axial resolution in soft tissue?","About 0.31 mm",["About 0.62 mm","About 1.54 mm","About 0.15 mm"],"λ = 0.31 mm; SPL = 2 × 0.31 = 0.62 mm; axial resolution = 0.62 ÷ 2 ≈ 0.31 mm.","C"),
(R,"A 2 MHz pulse with 3 cycles. What is the axial resolution in soft tissue?","About 1.16 mm",["About 2.31 mm","About 0.77 mm","About 0.39 mm"],"λ = 1.54 ÷ 2 = 0.77 mm; SPL = 3 × 0.77 = 2.31 mm; axial resolution = 1.155 mm.","C"),
(R,"Which pulse change makes axial resolution better?","Fewer cycles per pulse (more damping)",["More cycles per pulse","A lower frequency","A wider beam"],"Fewer cycles and higher frequency both shorten the pulse, so axial resolution improves (a smaller number).","F"),
(R,"A smaller number for axial resolution means:","Better image detail",["Worse image detail","No change in detail","Deeper penetration"],"Resolution values are distances. The shorter the distance at which two reflectors can be separated, the better.","F"),
(R,"Can the sonographer change axial resolution by adjusting knobs during the exam?","Only by changing the transducer frequency",["Yes, with the gain control","Yes, with the depth control","Yes, with the focus control"],"Axial resolution depends on pulse length, which is set by the transducer. Choosing a higher-frequency transducer or setting is the only way.","F"),
(R,"Typical axial resolution in clinical imaging is about:","0.05 to 0.5 mm",["5 to 10 mm","1 to 2 cm","0.001 mm"],"Axial resolution is usually better than lateral resolution in clinical imaging.","F"),
(R,"Axial resolution and lateral resolution usually compare how?","Axial resolution is better (smaller) than lateral resolution",["Lateral is always better","They are always equal","Neither can be measured"],"Pulses are shorter than beams are wide, so axial resolution is typically finer.","F"),
# --- Lateral ---
(R,"Lateral resolution is the ability to separate two structures that lie:","Side by side, perpendicular to the beam",["One in front of the other along the beam","Above and below the slice","At different times"],"Also called angular, transverse or azimuthal resolution (LATA).","F"),
(R,"Lateral resolution equals:","The beam width at that depth",["Half the pulse length","The wavelength","The slice thickness"],"Two reflectors side by side are resolved only if the beam is narrower than the gap between them.","F"),
(R,"Where along the beam is lateral resolution best?","At the focus, where the beam is narrowest",["At the transducer face","Deep in the far field","Where the beam is widest"],"Placing the focal zone at or just below the area of interest gives the sharpest side-to-side detail.","F"),
(R,"Two side-by-side structures 2 mm apart lie at a depth where the beam is 4 mm wide. They will appear as:","One structure",["Two structures","Three structures","Nothing"],"If the gap is smaller than the beam width, both are in the beam at once and their echoes merge.","F"),
(R,"Which change improves lateral resolution in the far field?","A higher frequency",["A lower frequency","A smaller aperture","Less focusing"],"Higher frequency reduces far-field divergence, so the beam stays narrower at depth.","F"),
(R,"The sonographer can improve lateral resolution during a scan by:","Moving the focal zone to the area of interest",["Increasing overall gain","Increasing depth","Turning off focusing"],"Focus narrows the beam at the chosen depth.","F"),
(R,"A typical lateral resolution value is about:","1 to 5 mm (often worse than axial)",["0.01 mm","5 cm","Always better than axial"],"Beam widths are millimeters, so lateral resolution is usually several times worse than axial.","F"),
(R,"Point targets far from the focus appear stretched sideways because:","The beam is wider there, so each target is drawn as wide as the beam",["Axial resolution is worse there","The PRF is lower there","Of reverberation"],"A small reflector is displayed as wide as the beam that hits it.","C"),
# --- Elevational ---
(R,"Elevational resolution relates to:","The thickness of the imaging slice",["The length of the pulse","The frame rate","The number of grayscale shades"],"The beam has thickness perpendicular to the image plane. A thick slice blurs structures above and below the plane into the image.","F"),
(R,"Slice-thickness artifact is caused by:","Poor elevational resolution",["Poor axial resolution","Aliasing","High frame rate"],"Echoes from just outside the image plane are included, often filling anechoic structures with false echoes.","F"),
(R,"Elevational resolution is improved most by:","1.5D or 2D arrays that focus in the elevational plane",["Raising the gain","Reducing depth","Using a linear sequential array"],"Standard 1D arrays have a fixed lens in elevation. Extra rows of elements allow electronic elevational focusing.","F"),
# --- Temporal ---
(R,"Temporal resolution is the ability to:","Show moving structures accurately in time",["Separate structures along the beam","Separate structures side by side","Show shades of gray"],"Temporal resolution depends on frame rate. More frames per second show rapid motion, such as valve leaflets, more accurately.","F"),
(R,"Temporal resolution is determined by:","Frame rate",["Pulse length","Beam width","Number of bits per pixel"],"A higher frame rate means a shorter time between pictures, giving better temporal resolution.","F"),
(R,"Which change improves temporal resolution?","Decreasing imaging depth",["Increasing depth","Adding focal zones","Increasing sector width"],"Shallower depth shortens the time to build each scan line, so more frames are made each second.","F"),
(R,"Which change lowers the frame rate?","Increasing line density",["Narrowing the sector","Decreasing depth","Using a single focal zone"],"More scan lines per frame means each frame takes longer to build.","F"),
(R,"Turning on color Doppler usually lowers the frame rate because:","Each color line needs several pulses (the packet) in addition to the B-mode lines",["Color uses a lower frequency","Color raises the depth","Color turns off focusing"],"Narrowing the color box and reducing packet size help restore frame rate.","F"),
(R,"Frame rate for a sector 15 cm deep with 100 lines per frame is about:","51 frames per second",["5 frames per second","100 frames per second","513 frames per second"],"Each line takes about 13 µs per cm of depth: 15 × 13 µs = 195 µs. 100 lines = 19.5 ms per frame, about 51 frames per second.","C"),
(R,"There is a trade-off between temporal resolution and:","Spatial resolution (line density) and depth",["Axial resolution only","Transducer frequency only","Gain"],"More lines or deeper imaging improves detail or coverage but slows the frame rate.","F"),
(R,"To image a fast-moving valve leaflet, the sonographer should:","Narrow the sector, reduce depth and use one focal zone",["Widen the sector and add focal zones","Increase depth to show the whole heart","Turn on color Doppler"],"These steps raise frame rate. M-mode gives the best temporal resolution of all.","F"),
(R,"Which mode has the best temporal resolution?","M-mode",["2D imaging","Color Doppler","3D imaging"],"M-mode samples one line thousands of times per second, so it captures very rapid motion.","F"),
# --- Contrast and spatial (pixel) ---
(R,"Contrast resolution is the ability to:","Tell apart echoes of slightly different strengths as different shades of gray",["Separate two reflectors along the beam","Show motion","Separate structures side by side"],"It depends on the number of bits per pixel and on processing such as dynamic range.","F"),
(R,"Contrast resolution is improved by:","More bits per pixel",["Fewer bits per pixel","Larger pixels","A lower frame rate"],"Each extra bit doubles the number of available gray shades. With n bits there are 2ⁿ shades.","F"),
(R,"A memory with 6 bits per pixel can display how many shades of gray?","64",["6","12","32"],"2⁶ = 64 shades.","F"),
(R,"Spatial resolution of the display improves with:","Higher pixel density (more, smaller pixels)",["Fewer pixels","More bits per pixel","Lower frame rate"],"More pixels per inch show finer detail. Bits per pixel affect contrast, not spatial detail.","F"),
(R,"Speckle in an ultrasound image is:","An interference pattern from many tiny scatterers, not true tissue texture",["True anatomy at the cellular level","Aliasing","Reverberation"],"Constructive and destructive interference from small scatterers creates a grainy pattern. Compounding and speckle reduction smooth it.","C"),
(R,"Spatial compounding improves image quality by:","Combining frames taken from several beam angles, reducing speckle and shadowing",["Raising the frame rate","Shortening the pulse","Removing harmonics"],"Averaging views from different angles smooths speckle and fills in shadows, but lowers frame rate.","C"),
(R,"Increasing transducer frequency affects resolution and penetration how?","Improves axial and lateral resolution but reduces penetration",["Improves penetration but worsens resolution","Improves only temporal resolution","Has no effect"],"Higher frequency shortens pulses and narrows far-field beams, but attenuation rises with frequency.","F"),
(R,"Which type of resolution is also called azimuthal resolution?","Lateral",["Axial","Elevational","Temporal"],"Lateral resolution names: lateral, angular, transverse, azimuthal (LATA).","F"),
(R,"Which type of resolution is also called range or radial resolution?","Axial",["Lateral","Elevational","Contrast"],"Axial resolution names: longitudinal, axial, range, radial, depth (LARRD).","F"),
(R,"Harmonic imaging improves lateral resolution mainly because:","The harmonic beam is narrower than the fundamental beam",["It increases the PRF","It shortens the pulse","It uses a lower frequency"],"Harmonics form mostly in the intense center of the beam, so the effective beam is narrower.","C"),
(R,"A smaller (narrower) transducer aperture at a fixed frequency will:","Shorten the near zone and increase far-field divergence",["Lengthen the near zone","Reduce divergence","Improve lateral resolution at depth"],"Near-zone length = D² ÷ 4λ, so a smaller diameter brings the focus closer and the far field spreads more.","C"),
(R,"Increasing the number of cycles in a pulse from 2 to 4, at the same frequency, makes axial resolution:","Twice as large a number (worse)",["Half as large (better)","Unchanged","Four times better"],"Doubling the cycles doubles the spatial pulse length, and axial resolution is half of SPL.","C"),
(T,"The wire connecting the transducer to the machine carries:","Electrical signals in both directions: excitation voltages out and echo voltages back",["Sound waves","Light pulses","Only power, not signals"],"The crystal converts between electrical and sound energy at the transducer, so the cable carries electrical signals only.","F"),
(T,"Why is the crystal thickness set to half a wavelength (in the crystal)?","So the crystal resonates most efficiently at its main frequency",["To match impedance with skin","To reduce grating lobes","To set the PRF"],"A half-wavelength-thick plate resonates naturally, so the transducer's main frequency depends on thickness.","C"),
(T,"Which transducer is most likely to show grating lobe artifacts?","A phased array",["A single-element mechanical transducer","A CW pencil probe","None: grating lobes do not occur"],"Grating lobes are extra beams caused by the regular spacing of array elements, especially when steering at wide angles.","F"),
(T,"Pencil (non-imaging) CW probes are used in echo because they:","Have a small footprint and high sensitivity for high-velocity jets from windows such as the right sternal border",["Produce 2D images","Have excellent range resolution","Use very high frequencies"],"Dedicated CW probes (often 2 MHz) are sensitive and easy to angle, useful for aortic stenosis jets.","F"),
(R,"Which type of resolution would best separate two thin valve leaflets lying one in front of the other along the beam?","Axial",["Lateral","Elevational","Temporal"],"Structures aligned along the beam are separated by axial resolution, which depends on pulse length.","F"),
(R,"Which type of resolution is improved by placing the focal zone at the level of interest?","Lateral",["Axial","Temporal","Contrast"],"Focusing narrows the beam at that depth. It does not change pulse length.","F"),
(R,"Write zoom (pre-processing zoom) improves resolution compared with read zoom because it:","Rescans the region of interest with more scan lines and pixels",["Simply enlarges existing pixels","Lowers the frame rate to zero","Changes the transducer frequency"],"Write zoom acquires new, finer data for the chosen area. Read zoom only magnifies stored data, so pixels look larger.","C"),
(R,"Persistence (frame averaging) improves image smoothness but can reduce:","Temporal resolution, by blurring rapid motion",["Axial resolution","Penetration","Contrast resolution"],"Averaging several frames reduces noise but smears fast-moving structures such as valves.","F"),
]
